Installing VLHE
===============

There are two ways to use VLHE:

  1. BUILD AND INSTALL IT from this source tree - below.
  2. RUN IT PORTABLE from an unpacked prebuilt folder - nothing is
     installed and nothing on the system is changed. See "Portable"
     at the end.


1. Building from source
-----------------------

    ./configure
    make
    make install        (as root)

configure looks at the machine and says what it can build. What it
finds decides what is built and, for the modules, which kernel's
/lib/modules they install into; running make without it builds
everything on the defaults. Its options:

    --prefix=DIR            install under DIR (default /usr/local)
    --kerneldir=DIR         the kernel source tree (default /usr/src/linux)
    --without-modules       build the programs only
    --without-gui           no control centre (no GTK 1.2 needed)
    --with-modversions      force MODVERSIONS on  - normally read from
    --without-modversions   force it off          - the running kernel

THE KERNEL MODULES need the source tree of the kernel you are running,
configured. On Corel Linux 1.2 that is the kernel-source-2.2.16
package from the install CD, which holds the tree as a tarball in
/usr/src. It unpacks unconfigured; the running kernel's configuration
is /boot/config-2.2.16. As root:

    cd /usr/src
    tar xzf kernel-source-2.2.16.tar.gz
    ln -s kernel-source-2.2.16 linux      (if there is no linux yet)
    cd linux
    cp /boot/config-2.2.16 .config
    make oldconfig dep

Copying the configuration in is not enough by itself: make oldconfig
dep writes the headers the modules are built against, and configure
refuses a tree without them and prints these commands. On Red Hat
Linux 6.0 the kernel-source package is already configured but has no
.config - configure reads the tree's include/linux/autoconf.h instead
and says so. Point --kerneldir at any other tree. configure checks that
the tree is a 2.2 kernel with module support, that it has the sound
driver's source, and whether the running kernel uses MODVERSIONS. If
the tree is for a different kernel than the one running, it says so:
the modules will then load only on that other kernel. GCC 3 or later
cannot build against a 2.2 tree; use the system's own compiler (GCC
2.95, egcs or 2.7).

ANY 2.2 KERNEL WILL DO, from 2.2.0 on, with one thing missing before
2.2.16: that is the release that added the CD-ROM packet interface, so
on an older kernel the virtual CD-ROM mounts and plays but CD rippers
(cdparanoia, cdda2wav) and Video CD players cannot read it. configure
says so when it finds such a tree. Corel Linux 1.2's own kernel is
2.2.16, so nothing is missing there.

THE CONTROL CENTRE needs GTK 1.2 and GLib 1.2 development files.
Corel Linux ships the runtime libraries but not these, so on Corel
configure reports "control centre: NO" - install VLHE's package, whose
control centre is prebuilt, or install libgtk1.2-dev and
libglib1.2-dev. A portable folder's vlhe.gtk cannot stand in: it works
only with that folder's own modules and daemons.

DEBIAN PACKAGES INSTEAD OF make install: after ./configure,

    make deb            (as root, or with fakeroot installed)

builds vlhe_<version>-1_i386.deb and vlhe-<kernel>-mod_<version>-1_i386.deb
for the kernel configure found; install them with dpkg -i. A machine
that boots two kernels builds once for each (configure --kerneldir)
and installs both module packages. make deb MAINT="Name <address>"
names who built them.

THE TESTS ARE NOT IN THIS TARBALL. make check, make check-all and the
other development targets live in host/, which only VLHE's git tree
carries; a release build needs none of them.

WHAT make install PUTS WHERE:

    $prefix/sbin/        vsoundd vdiscd vmidid    the daemons
                         vlhe                     the command-line tool
                         vlhe.gtk                 the control centre
                         setup-vlhe (+ its menu and strings files)
    $prefix/bin/         vlhe, vlhe.gtk           links to the above
                         smf2wav                  the MIDI renderer
    /lib/modules/<kernel>/misc/
                         vsound.o vdisc.o vmidi.o
    $prefix/share/vlhe/  vlhe-init                the boot script
                         vlhe-account             makes the vlhe account
                         vlhe-help.txt            the control centre's
                                                  help, as plain text
    $prefix/man/         man1/ vlhe vlhe.gtk smf2wav sysinfo-vlhe
                         man5/ vlhe.conf          the manual pages
                         man8/ setup-vlhe vsoundd vdiscd vmidid
    $prefix/share/vlhe/tools/
                         sysinfo-vlhe             a report to send if
                                                  something goes wrong
    /usr/X11R6/share/applnk/Applications/Multimedia/vlhe.kdelnk
                         the KDE menu entry, beside KMix and KsCD
    /usr/X11R6/share/applnk/Applications/System/vlhe-root.kdelnk
                         the same as root, beside Corel's File
                         Manager (root)
    /usr/X11R6/share/icons/vlhe.xpm, icons/mini/vlhe.xpm
                         its icons (the entry and icons installed only
                         where KDE is, and with the control centre)

That is Corel's layout. make install looks for the menus and says
where it put each entry: on Red Hat Linux 6.0 KDE's are in
/usr/share/applnk/Multimedia (icons in /usr/share/icons), and GNOME
gets vlhe.desktop in /usr/share/gnome/apps/Multimedia (icon in
/usr/share/pixmaps). The root entry needs Corel's kvt_kappsu to ask
for the password, so where there is none it is left out - start
vlhe.gtk from a root shell there. Both entries name $prefix/bin in
full, so they work even when it is not on the PATH.

THE PATH. $prefix/bin must be on the PATH for vlhe and vlhe.gtk to be
found by name. Corel's is; on Red Hat 6.0 /usr/local/bin was not, and
vlhe was "command not found" after a clean install.
make install says so when the shell it ran in cannot find them, and
gives the line to add - or configure with --prefix=/usr.

ON RED HAT the daemons, which run as the vlhe account, need three
things it does not set up: the <sound> line in
/etc/security/console.perms made to read
    <console> 0666 <sound>     0666 root
(make install says so); SoundFonts and disc images kept outside home
directories, which are private there (or chmod 711 the home); and the
card loaded at boot - "alias sound <driver>" in /etc/conf.modules,
which sndconfig writes.

then runs depmod and makes the vlhe account (below). For packaging,
make install DESTDIR=/stage installs into /stage instead and leaves
both to the package.


Starting VLHE at boot
---------------------

make install does NOT add VLHE to the boot sequence. To have it load
at every boot, as root, after make install:

    make install-boot

It copies the init script into place and links it in - to start at
boot, and to stop at shutdown, at reboot and on entering single-user
mode. It knows two layouts. Debian's - Corel Linux 1.2, and Debian 2.2
"potato" has the same directories, though VLHE has not been run there:

    /etc/init.d/vlhe
    /etc/rcS.d/S60vlhe
    /etc/rc0.d/K40vlhe  /etc/rc1.d/K40vlhe  /etc/rc6.d/K40vlhe

and Red Hat's (Red Hat Linux 6.0), used where there is no /etc/rcS.d:

    /etc/rc.d/init.d/vlhe
    /etc/rc.d/rc2.d/S60vlhe ... /etc/rc.d/rc5.d/S60vlhe
    /etc/rc.d/rc0.d/K40vlhe  rc1.d/K40vlhe  rc6.d/K40vlhe

On Red Hat the script also keeps /var/lock/subsys/vlhe while VLHE is
up, because Red Hat's boot scripts stop a service at shutdown only if
that file exists.

make uninstall-boot takes them out again, and removes only links and
a script that are VLHE's. Neither runs where the vlhe package is
installed - the package already does this - or on a machine with
neither layout.

TO DO IT BY HAND, make install prints the commands for this machine at
the end. On Corel or Debian they are:

    cp $prefix/share/vlhe/vlhe-init /etc/init.d/vlhe
    update-rc.d vlhe start 60 S . stop 40 0 1 6 .

On Red Hat, copy it to /etc/rc.d/init.d/vlhe and make the S60 and K40
links listed above, each pointing at ../init.d/vlhe. Anywhere else,
have the boot run "$prefix/share/vlhe/vlhe-init start" once local
filesystems are mounted and /var/run has been cleaned, and
"$prefix/share/vlhe/vlhe-init stop" at shutdown.

S60 is after every device driver is up and after the boot-time
cleaning of /var/run, which would otherwise delete VLHE's running
state. The K40 links stop it before the system shuts down, reboots or
goes to single user, which puts /dev/dsp back the way it was. Without
them the /dev/dsp redirect outlives a shutdown and audio programs fail
until VLHE loads again.

A single-user boot (the "Single_User" entry at the boot prompt, or
"single" or "1" typed there) does not start VLHE. Run
/etc/init.d/vlhe start (/etc/rc.d/init.d/vlhe start on Red Hat) by
hand if you want it there.

What it loads at boot is each component's "LoadAtBoot" setting in
/etc/vlhe.conf - set with setup-vlhe. The control centre's "Include
in load" boxes are separate: they decide what its Load button does,
and never change what boots.

The control centre as an ordinary user
--------------------------------------

Anyone can change settings. An ordinary user's File > Save keeps the
machine settings in a draft (vlhe-draft.conf, beside their own
settings) which root brings in with File > Load Configuration; the
system's own /etc/vlhe.conf needs root to write. Loading and
unloading modules needs root. There are two ways:

  - run vlhe.gtk as root: Applications > System > VLHE Control
    Centre (root) in the KDE menu, which asks for the root password.
    From a terminal, the same thing is
        kappsu "VLHE" /usr/bin/vlhe.gtk
    (kappsu takes a title, then the command). su does NOT work for
    the window: su - drops your DISPLAY, and plain su gives root no
    key to your X display ("Invalid MIT-MAGIC-COOKIE-1 key"). kappsu
    lets root onto the display while the program runs. Run as root,
    VLHE ignores its VLHE_* test variables and MODPATH and uses a
    fixed system PATH. Or
  - use the setuid build, which runs as the user and asks for the root
    password (through KDE's kcheckpass) when root is needed. It is a
    PORTABLE test build and is never installed: build it, with the
    control centre enabled, with

        make portable SETUID=1

    which leaves it as build/portable/vlhe.gtk.su. Copy it into the
    portable folder beside vlhe.gtk and, as root, chown root vlhe.gtk.su
    and then chmod 4755 vlhe.gtk.su - setuid takes effect only on a
    file root owns. A plain `make portable' does not make it.


The vlhe account and group
--------------------------

An installed VLHE runs its daemons (vsoundd, vmidid, vdiscd) as a
system account named vlhe, not as root. Nobody logs in as it. If the
account is missing, the daemons are started as root and the load says
so. Its group decides who may use the virtual drives and talk to the
daemons: the drives /dev/vdisc0 upwards, /dev/vdiscctl, the daemons'
control channels in /var/run/vlhe/ctl and the drive state in
/var/lib/vlhe/state all belong to it. /var/lib/vlhe and /var/run/vlhe
themselves stay root's.

make install (and the package) makes the account with Corel's own
adduser and adds the desktop users to the group - those in the audio
group, which is how Corel marks the person at the desk. Where adduser
is not Debian's (on Red Hat it is another name for useradd) it uses
groupadd, useradd and gpasswd instead. Root is not added; it needs
nothing from the group. The account itself is put in audio so the
pump can open the sound card. To let someone else use the drives:

    adduser NAME vlhe       (as root; they log in again after)
    gpasswd -a NAME vlhe    (the same, on Red Hat)

What it changes for you: the disc server opens an image with an
ordinary user's rights, so an image you load must be readable by
others - on Corel it is, unless you chmod 600 it, and then the
control centre says why it cannot be opened. The same goes for
SoundFonts: a font under /root (which only root can read) will not
load; keep fonts somewhere readable, such as /usr/local/share.

A portable folder uses none of this: it runs the daemons as whoever
pressed Load and changes nothing on the system.

The account script is $prefix/share/vlhe/vlhe-account; it is safe to
run again. Uninstalling leaves the account; remove it with
userdel vlhe, then groupdel vlhe, if you want it gone.


Settings
--------

    /etc/vlhe.conf              the machine's settings
    ~/.vlhe/                    each user's own (window, viewer, fonts,
                                renders), and an ordinary user's draft
    /etc/vlhe/defaults.conf     what setup-vlhe first configured - used
                                when /etc/vlhe.conf is missing or lost,
                                to start each user's own file, and by
                                File > Reset Settings

setup-vlhe writes /etc/vlhe.conf from a menu on the console. In the
control centre, OK and Apply hold changes for the session - its Load
uses them straight away - and only File > Save Configuration writes
them to disk. Quitting with unsaved changes asks first.


Uninstalling
------------

As root, from the source tree it was installed from (so configure's
--prefix matches):

    make uninstall

make install keeps a list of every file it placed, menu entries
included, in $prefix/share/vlhe/installed-files, and make uninstall
removes exactly those - nothing it did not put there. (An install
made before the list existed is uninstalled from VLHE's known file
names instead.) If VLHE is running it stops it first, with
vlhe apply -u, and removes nothing if that fails; it takes out the
boot links if make install-boot made them, and runs depmod after the
modules go.

It keeps what a reinstall would want back, and says so:
/etc/vlhe.conf and /etc/vlhe/ (the settings), each user's ~/.vlhe/,
the state and change journal in /var/lib/vlhe, /var/run/vlhe and
/var/log/vlhe, the drives' device nodes /dev/vdisc* and /dev/vdiscctl,
and the vlhe account (userdel vlhe, then groupdel vlhe). Remove those
by hand for a clean machine.

Installing again over an existing install is also fine - make install
replaces every file - but make uninstall first leaves no stray menu
entry behind if the menus are now found somewhere else.

Where VLHE came from its .deb packages, remove it with dpkg -r
instead; make uninstall refuses there.


2. Portable
-----------

A portable VLHE is a folder holding the prebuilt programs, the modules
for one kernel, and a file named PORTABLE. Unpack it anywhere and run
the control centre from it:

    cd vlhe-1.0-portable-i386
    ./vlhe.gtk

The programs you run are at the top of the folder; the rest is in
three folders beside them:

    modules/    vsound.o vdisc.o vmidi.o
    daemons/    vsoundd vdiscd vmidid
    tools/      smf2wav; sysinfo-vlhe (a report to send if something
                goes wrong)

The layout is fixed. A portable folder uses only its own modules and
programs, even on a machine where VLHE is also installed, and an
installed VLHE never uses a portable folder's.

WHICH ONE A PROGRAM IS WAS DECIDED WHEN IT WAS BUILT: `make portable'
builds the portable programs, `make' the installed ones. The PORTABLE
file only says what the folder is - deleting it changes nothing. (It
used to switch the folder to the system's /etc and /var while still
running the folder's programs; that is gone.)

It keeps its settings in that folder (or in ~/.vlhe if the folder
cannot be written, after asking) and installs nothing: no files
outside the folder, no boot entry, no change to /etc. The boot script
never starts a portable copy. Loading the modules still needs root,
and lasts until they are unloaded or the machine is restarted. While
loaded it does change the running system as an installed VLHE does -
it makes the drives' device nodes, points /dev/dsp at the mixer, and
keeps the daemons' process IDs in /var/run/vlhe - and unloading puts
those back.

The prebuilt modules are for one kernel only - the folder says which.
On any other kernel, build them from source as above.

MAKING A PORTABLE FOLDER FROM THIS SOURCE: after ./configure,

    make modules
    make portable-dir PORTABLE_DIR=/some/folder

builds the portable programs and lays the folder out as above, with
the modules for the kernel configure found. It writes into an empty
or new folder, or one that is already a portable folder, and deletes
nothing.
